WORKER documentation, last updated on 10 Sep 2026

A worker is a thread that does something slow so that the server does not
have to stop while it happens.  The IRC core stays single-threaded; only
the work around it moves.

This is the implementation of doc/proposals/002-multithreaded-event-loop.md,
option B.


READ THIS FIRST
===============

There is exactly one rule, and everything else in this file follows from it:

  A WORKER THREAD NEVER TOUCHES CORE STATE.

Not struct Client, not struct Channel, not the hash tables, not
CurrentTime, not any global.  Not MyMalloc() and not MyFree().  Not
log_write(), not Debug(), not send_reply(), not sendcmdto_one() or any of
the sendto_* family.  Not findNUser(), not FindUser(), not feature_int().

The reason is not style.  The core keeps its clients and channels on free
lists, its buffers in file-scope statics, and its time in a global that the
main thread rewrites after every pass through the event engine.  None of it
is locked, because for twenty-five years nothing else was running.  A
worker thread that reaches into any of it corrupts memory -- quietly, under
load, in a way that shows up as a crash somewhere else entirely, hours
later, on a server you cannot attach a debugger to.

What a worker thread MAY call is short enough to list in full:

  worker_alloc()      allocate memory
  worker_free()       release it
  worker_log()        write a line to the server log
  worker_submit()     hand another task to the pool
  worker_post()       hand a result to the main thread (dedicated workers)
  worker_stopping()   has somebody asked this worker to stop?
  worker_stop_fd()    a descriptor that becomes readable when they have
  worker_name()

  the system allocator and the system's own thread-safe library functions

  pure functions that operate on a context the caller supplies.  SHA1Init(),
  SHA1Update() and SHA1Final() qualify: they keep no state of their own.
  Anything that returns a pointer to a static buffer does not.

Everything a worker needs, it receives copied into the task.  Everything it
produces, it returns copied in the task.  That boundary is the only part of
the server that has to be audited for thread safety, and keeping it that
way is the whole point of the design.


TURNING IT ON
=============

Nothing runs until you ask for it.  Two features, both in
doc/readme.features:

  WORKER_THREADS      size of the pool.  0, the default, means no threads,
                      no queues, no pipe -- the server behaves exactly as it
                      did before workers existed.  Maximum 64.
  WORKER_QUEUE_MAX    how many tasks may be outstanding at once; further
                      submissions are refused until the queue drains.
                      Default 1024.

  features {
    "WORKER_THREADS" = "4";
  };

Both take effect on /SET and on /REHASH.  Raising WORKER_THREADS starts
threads; lowering it stops the surplus, waiting for whatever they are in
the middle of.

WORKER_THREADS = 0 is the master switch, and it is deliberately blunt: it
stops the pool AND every dedicated worker, and makes worker_submit() and
worker_spawn() fail until it is non-zero again.  Setting it back does not
bring dedicated workers back; the module that wanted one asks again.

/STATS M reports the pool, the dedicated workers, and how much work has
been submitted, completed and refused.


THE TWO SHAPES
==============

A TASK is a one-shot unit of work.  You fill in a struct WorkTask, submit
it, and get called back in the main thread when it is done.  The pool is a
fixed set of interchangeable threads; a task goes to whichever one is free.
This is the shape for a database query, a hash, an HTTP request, publishing
to a message bus -- anything with a beginning and an end.

A DEDICATED WORKER is a thread with a loop of its own that stays up for as
long as you want it.  This is the shape for something that is not a series
of short tasks: a listening socket, a subscription, an embedded service.
It hands results to the main thread with worker_post(), which puts them on
the same reply queue a finished task uses.

Both end up in the same place: a callback running in the main thread, where
core state is safe to touch.


A TASK, END TO END
==================

  #include "module.h"
  #include "worker.h"

  struct MyRequest { int n; char text[64]; };
  struct MyResult  { int answer; };

  /* --- in a worker thread --- */
  static void my_work(struct WorkTask *task)
  {
    const struct MyRequest *req = task->wt_in;
    struct MyResult *res = worker_alloc(sizeof(*res));

    if (!res) {                       /* say so rather than pretending */
      task->wt_status = -1;
      return;
    }

    res->answer = something_slow(req->n);

    task->wt_out = res;
    task->wt_out_len = sizeof(*res);
    task->wt_status = 0;
  }

  /* --- back in the main thread --- */
  static void my_done(struct WorkTask *task)
  {
    struct Client *sptr = worker_task_client(task);
    const struct MyResult *res = task->wt_out;

    if (!sptr)                        /* they left while we worked */
      return;

    if (task->wt_status) {
      sendcmdto_one(&me, CMD_NOTICE, sptr, "%C :it failed", sptr);
      return;
    }

    sendcmdto_one(&me, CMD_NOTICE, sptr, "%C :answer %d", sptr, res->answer);
  }

  /* --- submitting, from a command handler or a hook --- */
  static int m_mycmd(struct Client *cptr, struct Client *sptr,
                     int parc, char *parv[])
  {
    struct WorkTask *task = worker_task_new(my_work, my_done);
    struct MyRequest *req;

    if (!task)
      return 0;

    if (!(req = worker_alloc(sizeof(*req)))) {
      worker_task_free(task);
      return 0;
    }

    req->n = atoi(parv[1]);
    task->wt_in = req;
    task->wt_in_len = sizeof(*req);

    worker_task_set_client(task, sptr);

    if (!module_submit_work(my_module, task)) {
      worker_task_free(task);           /* still ours: say why */
      sendcmdto_one(&me, CMD_NOTICE, sptr, "%C :no workers available", sptr);
      return 0;
    }

    return 0;                           /* the answer follows, later */
  }

Note what the submit does NOT do: it does not wait.  The command handler
returns immediately, the server carries on serving everybody else, and the
answer arrives whenever it arrives.  A user who expects a reply gets an
acknowledgement now and the result later.


WHO OWNS WHAT
=============

  worker_task_new()   gives you a task.  It is yours.
  worker_submit()     on success, the task is the server's.  On failure it
                      is still yours, and you free it.
  after wt_done       the server frees the task.  Never keep the pointer.

The payload follows the task.  By default the server calls worker_free() on
wt_in and wt_out after wt_done has run, which is right for anything
allocated with worker_alloc().  If the payload owns something else -- a
descriptor, a nested allocation, a handle from a third-party library --
supply wt_free and release it there.  wt_free runs on every path, including
when the task is cancelled without ever having been worked on.

Use worker_alloc() and worker_free(), not MyMalloc() and MyFree().  This is
the easiest rule in the file to break out of habit and one of the worst to
break: the core's pools are plain linked lists with no locking, and two
threads pushing onto one produces corruption that surfaces nowhere near the
code that caused it.


NEVER STORE A struct Client *
=============================

A task outlives the command that created it, by design -- that is what it
is for.  The user who asked may be gone by the time the answer is ready.
And a struct Client is not freed to the system; it goes back on a free list
and is handed to the next connection.  A pointer stored across a task is a
use-after-free waiting for a slow query.

So the API does not accept one:

  worker_task_set_client(task, sptr);        /* main thread */
  ...
  struct Client *sptr = worker_task_client(task);   /* main thread */
  if (!sptr)
    return;                                  /* they left; nothing to do */

What the task actually stores is the numnick plus the time the client
connected.  The lookup checks both, because numnicks are reused -- the
allocator walks the whole 2^18 space before it comes back round, but
"eventually" is not "never", and the failure it would cause is answering
one user's question to a different user.

Both calls are main-thread only.  A worker thread has no business knowing
which client the work is for; if it needs the nick, copy the nick into the
task.


A DEDICATED WORKER
==================

  static void my_main(struct Worker *worker, void *arg)
  {
    while (!worker_stopping(worker)) {
      fd_set r;
      int stopfd = worker_stop_fd(worker);

      FD_ZERO(&r);
      FD_SET(stopfd, &r);
      FD_SET(my_socket, &r);

      if (select(max + 1, &r, NULL, NULL, NULL) < 0)
        break;

      if (FD_ISSET(stopfd, &r))
        break;                       /* asked to stop */

      /* ... do the work, then hand it over ... */
      task = worker_task_new(NULL, my_report);   /* no wt_work: done already */
      task->wt_out = result;
      if (!worker_post(worker, task))
        worker_task_free(task);      /* queue full; drop it, don't block */
    }
  }

  /* in mi_init */
  my_worker = module_spawn_worker(mod, "myservice", my_main, NULL);
  if (!my_worker)
    return -1;                       /* workers are off; say so and refuse */

Wait on worker_stop_fd(), do not sleep.  worker_stop() writes a byte to
that descriptor and then joins the thread, so a worker parked in select()
stops immediately and a worker asleep for sixty seconds keeps the server
waiting for up to sixty seconds.

module_spawn_worker() may be called from mi_init even though the worker
subsystem has not started at that point -- a module loaded from a Module{}
block runs its mi_init while the configuration file is still being read.
The request is held and the thread starts once the configuration is known.
It still returns NULL when WORKER_THREADS is zero, so a module that needs a
thread can refuse to load and say why.

The server owns the worker and frees it when it stops it, which happens
when the module unloads, when WORKER_THREADS is set to zero, or when the
module asks with module_stop_worker().  The module's copy of the pointer is
stale from then on.  module_stop_worker() checks the handle against the
list of live workers before it reads anything through it, so calling it on
a worker the server already stopped returns zero and does nothing -- a
module that stops its thread from mi_fini, as worker_ticker.c does, is
always doing exactly that, because the unload stops the thread first.
Nothing else in the API accepts a stale handle: worker_name() and
worker_owner() on one read freed memory.


UNLOADING A MODULE THAT HAS WORK IN FLIGHT
==========================================

Unloading a module unmaps its code.  If a worker thread is executing that
code at the time, there is no clever way out: the server waits.

  worker: waiting for work in flight before unloading a module
  worker: still waiting after 2 seconds; the server is blocked until a
          worker returns
  worker: discarded 5 tasks belonging to an unloaded module

Everything that module owns goes with it -- tasks not yet started are
dropped, tasks running are waited for, results already finished are thrown
away rather than delivered (wt_done belongs to the module too), and its
dedicated workers are stopped and joined.

The server is unresponsive while this happens.  That is the cost of
unloading a module in the middle of a fifty-million-round hash, it is
logged so an operator can see why the server went quiet, and it is the
reason a wt_work that can run for minutes should check something and give
up early.

A wt_work that never returns hangs the server permanently.  There is no
timeout, because there is no safe way to enforce one: killing a thread
mid-allocation leaves the allocator broken.  Do not write one.


LOGGING AND TIME
================

log_write() and Debug() are not thread-safe.  From a worker thread use
worker_log(), which formats the line there and hands it to the main thread
to write, so it appears slightly late and is dropped rather than blocking
if the reply queue is full.

CurrentTime is a core global the main thread rewrites after every pass
through the event engine.  From a worker thread call time(NULL).


HOW THE MAIN THREAD FINDS OUT
=============================

A worker that finishes a task puts it on the reply queue and writes one
byte to a pipe.  The read end of that pipe is registered with the event
system as an ordinary Socket, exactly the way signal delivery has worked
since ircd_events.c was written.  The engine sees a readable descriptor,
the callback drains the queue, and every wt_done runs in the main thread
between commands.

None of the five event engines needed changing.  The byte is coalesced --
one is enough however many tasks are waiting -- so a busy pool does not
fill the pipe.


CHECKING YOUR WORK
==================

Build with ThreadSanitizer and run the unit test:

  cmake --preset tsan && cmake --build --preset tsan
  ctest --preset tsan -R worker_t

ircd/test/worker_t.c covers the boundary: work crossing in both directions,
the queue bound, the client reference surviving a quit and a reused
numnick, and a module unload taking exactly its own work.  Any change to
ircd/worker.c should be run under tsan several times, because a race that
appears one run in five is still a race.

When reviewing a module that uses workers, the check is short.  Read every
function that runs in a worker thread and ask of each line: does this touch
anything the main thread can also touch?  If the answer is anywhere other
than "no", it is a bug, whether or not it has caused one yet.


THE EXAMPLES
============

  modules/workers/worker_demo.c
                            adds /HASHPASS, which derives a key with
                            PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA1 in a pool thread; the
                            reference for worker_submit() and for the
                            client reference.  Ask it for a few million
                            rounds and watch the server keep answering.

  modules/workers/worker_ticker.c
                            starts a thread of its own that waits on its
                            stop descriptor and posts back every ten
                            seconds; the reference for worker_spawn(),
                            worker_post() and worker_stop_fd().


WHAT THIS IS NOT
================

The IRC core is still single-threaded.  Parsing, channels, the hash tables
and the P10 protocol all run in one thread on one core, and this does not
change that -- see section 3 of the proposal for why making it multi-
threaded is a different and much larger project.

What workers buy is that a slow thing no longer stops the server.  What
they do not buy is more throughput for IRC itself.
